<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>8.9.4: Solving More Quadratic Equations</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02227</md:content-id>
    <md:title>8.9.4: Solving More Quadratic Equations</md:title>
    <md:uuid>6e862cb5-1070-40a1-946e-9d4a9dbd51d3</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Cool Down (5 minutes)</title>
<title>Activity</title>
<para>Solve each equation by rewriting it in factored form and using the zero product property. Be prepared to explain your reasoning.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>\(x^2+ 12x + 11 = 0\)</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(x=-1\) and \(x=-11\)
  The equation \(x^2 + 12x + 11 = 0\) can be written as \((x + 11)(x + 1) = 0\), which means \(x + 11 = 0\) or \(x + 1 = 0\).</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>\(x^2− 3 = 1\)</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(x=-2\) and \(x=2\)
  The equation \(x^2− 3 = 1\) can be written as \(x^2 − 4 = 0\), which is \((x + 2)(x − 2) = 0\), which means \(x + 2 = 0\) or \(x − 2 = 0\).</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>\(x^2− 6x + 7 = -2\)</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(x=3\)
  The equation \(x^2− 6x + 7 = -2\) can be written as \(x^2− 6x + 9 = 0\), which is \((x − 3)(x − 3) = 0\), which means \(x − 3 = 0\).</para>
<title>Response to Student Thinking</title>
<title>Press Pause</title>
<para>If students are still making substantial errors with factoring, spend some time reviewing student work from cool downs and providing additional practice opportunities through practice problems.</para></section>
</content>
